Step 1 — Install Git and unzip
Git downloads your scripts from GitHub. Unzip extracts the downloaded toolkit.
sudo dnf install -y git unzip


You only need to install these once.
Step 2 — Download your GitHub repository
cd ~
git clone https://github.com/BikashKatuwal/Foghorn.git


Why? This creates a local Foghorn folder containing the files stored in your GitHub repository.

If you've already cloned it, update it instead:
cd ~/Foghorn
git pull


Step 3 — Enter the repository
cd ~/Foghorn
ls -la


You should see:
Foghorn-CCDC-Triage.zip


Step 4 — Extract the script
unzip -o Foghorn-CCDC-Triage.zip


Why? The script is inside the ZIP archive. The -o option overwrites previously extracted files with the archive's versions, so use it only if you want to replace your earlier extracted copies.

Verify:
ls -la foghorn-ccdc/


You should see ccdc-triage.sh and README.txt.
Step 5 — Run the complete CCDC investigation
sudo bash foghorn-ccdc/ccdc-triage.sh all


This runs your investigation toolkit and collects information on accounts, processes, services, ports, firewall rules, SSH, logs, and persistence.

It's designed to gather evidence without changing the system's security configuration.
Step 6 — Find the generated reports
Your script creates a new timestamped folder each time.

To find the latest one:
sudo ls -dt /root/foghorn-triage-*


To see its files without typing a long folder name:
sudo ls -lah "$(sudo ls -dt /root/foghorn-triage-* | head -1)"


Step 7 — Read the reports
Here are the most important reports for Foghorn:
# Listening ports
sudo cat "$(sudo ls -dt /root/foghorn-triage-* | head -1)/listeners.txt"

# Running services
sudo cat "$(sudo ls -dt /root/foghorn-triage-* | head -1)/running-services.txt"

# Administrator group members
sudo cat "$(sudo ls -dt /root/foghorn-triage-* | head -1)/wheel-members.txt"

# Network connections
sudo cat "$(sudo ls -dt /root/foghorn-triage-* | head -1)/connections.txt"

# Firewall settings
sudo cat "$(sudo ls -dt /root/foghorn-triage-* | head -1)/firewall.txt"


Quick reference
Command	Purpose
git clone	Download GitHub repository
git pull	Update existing repository
unzip	Extract ZIP
bash -n	Check script syntax
sudo bash ... all	Run complete investigation
ls	List files
cat	Display file contents
ss -tulpn	Identify listening network ports
systemctl	Inspect running services

The shortest way after the initial setup
Once the repository is cloned and extracted, you only need these two commands to generate a fresh report:
cd ~/Foghorn
sudo bash foghorn-ccdc/ccdc-triage.sh all


Then find its output:
sudo ls -dt /root/foghorn-triage-*
